# Token efficiency

Apply silently. Do not restate this rule or the user's ask before acting.

## Source of truth — read, don't regenerate

Before any component, endpoint, or file: read existing standards and apply them. Do **not** ask the user to re-paste rules already in the repo.

Canonical files (use what exists; names in prompts may be aliases):

- Visual: `frontend-design-directive.md`, skill `flowdesk-ui`, rule `frontend-horizon-2050.mdc`
- Frontend code: `FlowDesk-Frontend/docs/frontend-coding-standards.md`
- Backend: `FlowDesk-Backend/docs/standards/backend.md`
- Cross-cutting: `docs/cross-cutting.md`, `docs/engineering-standards.md`
- Mentor: `.cursor/rules/flowdesk-mentor.mdc`
- Requirements: `FlowDesk-Backend/docs/requirements.md`, `frd.md`

There is **no** `project-standards.md` — map that phrase to the files above.

## Responses

- Cite by filename/skill (`per flowdesk-ui`, `per frontend-coding-standards.md`). Quote at most 1–2 lines needed for the current task. Never paste a standard in full.
- Scope to exactly what was asked. No adjacent-file "consistency" rewrites unless asked.
- Confirm in **one line**, then do the work. Do not echo the full instruction list.
- If required context is **missing** from all docs: say so, then default to **(a)** add a reusable standard/skill entry for anything that will recur; **(b)** one-off only if they choose it.
- If the user re-explains the same rule across prompts: flag it and offer to add a skill/doc entry instead.
